    en: {
      title: 'Official advertising: US$110 million with no law setting criteria',
      dek: 'The State and its companies spent over $4.5 billion pesos on advertising between 2020 and 2024, per Observacom, and Uruguay still has no law governing how it is distributed.',
      contexto: 'Official advertising is the public money that ministries, agencies and state-owned companies spend on placements in the media. Uruguay has no law setting allocation criteria: each agency decides which outlets it contracts and for how much. In August 2015, representatives of every party with parliamentary representation, together with the Centre for Archives and Access to Public Information (CAinfo), submitted a bill to the Chamber of Deputies to regulate the production, planning, contracting and distribution of official advertising and to reserve at least 30% of the budget for media and productions from the interior, as Presidencia reported. That bill was never passed. In December 2025 Frente Amplio deputies filed a new version.',
      hallazgo: 'According to an analysis by Observacom, led by Gustavo Gómez, based on freedom-of-information requests to the Office of Planning and Budget (OPP) and published by Caras y Caretas on 11 June 2025, between 2020 and 2024 the State spent more than $4.5 billion pesos (some US$110 million) on official advertising: 80% was executed by state-owned companies —BROU, Antel, UTE and the state insurance bank BSE— and the remaining 20% was split among state bodies such as Presidencia, ministries, the Judiciary and the University of the Republic. The Ministry of Tourism was the largest advertiser among state bodies, international campaigns included. Around 95% of the spending stayed in Montevideo and 5% went to the interior. In a second instalment, on 23 June 2025, the same outlet reported that state companies spent 54% more in 2024 than in 2019 (US$22.7 million versus US$14.8 million) —Antel +50%, UTE +72%, BROU +76%— and that five advertisers (BROU, Antel, Tourism, UTE and BSE) accounted for about 84% of the period\'s total.',
      statusNote: 'On 25 December 2025 Frente Amplio deputies, with Javier Umpiérrez as lead author, filed a bill requiring audience measurement before advertising is allocated, reserving at least 30% of nationally scoped official advertising for media with production based in the interior, earmarking 3% a year for audience and readership studies and creating a promotion fund financed with 1% of official advertising budgets, according to la diaria and Montevideo Portal. Fabián Werner, of CAinfo, noted the debate has been running for almost 20 years. As of this file\'s closing (August 2026) there is no record of developments after 26 December 2025 on its parliamentary treatment.',
      porQueImporta: 'With no legal criteria, the decision of which outlet gets paid and how much rests on each official\'s discretion, using money from utility bills and taxes. Beyond spending transparency, what is at stake is the independence of the media that report on the very State funding them.',
      caveat: 'The published figures come from freedom-of-information requests to the OPP, not from a single register. Much of the advertising is contracted through media agencies and framework agreements, so it may not appear labelled as advertising in the state procurement search.',
    },
